# Shared step contract for the build pipeline.
#
# READ BY ALL THREE RUNNERS — scripts/pipeline.sh (Linux), packaging/windows/pipeline.ps1
# (Windows) and packaging/macos/pipeline.sh (macOS). It is the one definition of what the
# pipeline IS, and it exists because there was none: POLICY § Build pipeline was written
# as instructions to a reader rather than as a contract a program could consume, so each
# platform encoded its own reading of it. pipeline.ps1 numbered its steps 1-5, 9, plus
# unnumbered staging steps, and inserted a 4b that POLICY does not mention. Nothing
# detected the divergence, because nothing had an opinion about what the step list was.
#
# DERIVATION, NOT COMPARISON. Every runner must BUILD its step list by reading this file,
# and `--list-steps` / `-ListSteps` prints exactly that derived list. The distinction is
# load-bearing and was paid for one level down: `--list-scan` proved the two lint ports
# agreed WITH EACH OTHER and for a long time proved neither honoured
# lint-references.scan — it printed a set some checks did not consume (ScrAP-207). It
# worked only because both ports genuinely read the contract, so agreement and
# conformance happened to coincide. Where a port RESTATES instead of deriving, a clean
# diff proves only that two restatements match, which two people copying the same wrong
# list also achieve. Comparison proves agreement; derivation proves conformance.
#
# THE CONTRACT PINS INTENT, NOT COMMANDS. `intent` is the invariant; `cmd.<platform>` is
# how that platform satisfies it. This is deliberate and was measured: `xmllint`, `bash`
# and `cargo llvm-cov` are all absent from the PowerShell PATH, so POLICY step 8 as
# written (`xmllint --noout`) cannot run on Windows — but its intent is satisfiable there
# with a built-in and no new dependency. A contract of COMMANDS would have forced Windows
# to install xmllint or file a false non-applicable declaration, and both are wrong.
#
# A NON-APPLICABLE STEP IS DECLARED IN THE RUN OUTPUT, NOT OMITTED. An unexplained
# asymmetry is what the next contributor "fixes" into a bug. And the declaration must
# reach the pipeline's USER: pipeline.ps1 declared step 6 at runtime with its reason but
# declared steps 7 and 8 only in a source comment, so someone reading the run saw
# `1, 2, 3, 4, 4b, 5, 6, 9` with no indication that 7 and 8 existed. A declaration only a
# code reader can find is not a declaration.
#
# RECORD THE SEMANTIC REASON, NEVER THE TOOLING ONE. `na.<platform>` carries a KIND
# because the two kinds are acted on differently: `permanent` is true even with perfect
# tooling; `tooling` is fixable by installing something. Step 6 is the worked example and
# shows why it matters — it is non-applicable on Windows for BOTH reasons (semantically,
# unix-only code shifts numerator and denominator so a Windows figure can legitimately
# fall below the floor with no regression; incidentally, cargo llvm-cov is not installed
# there). The contract cites the SEMANTIC one, because if it cited the tooling one the
# first person to run a single `cargo install` would enable a gate that produces a
# meaningless number — and a meaningless gate that runs is worse than one honestly
# declared off.
#
# GRAMMAR. Every line is `keyword  key  value...` — the value is the remainder of the
# line, so it may contain spaces. Uniform arity is deliberate: both a POSIX shell and
# PowerShell must parse this identically, and a format with per-keyword arity is where
# two parsers quietly disagree. Compound keywords (`cmd.linux`) keep the platform inside
# the keyword rather than adding a fourth field.
#
#   platform  <id>   <display name>
#   step      <ord>  <id>                  ordinal fixes the run order
#   intent    <id>   <what must be true>   the invariant; never a command
#   verdict   <id>   exit | marker:<text>  how the step is judged
#   class     <id>   required | informational | review | packaging
#   cmd.<plat> <id>  <command line>        how THIS platform satisfies the intent
#                                          MAY carry leading `NAME=VALUE ` env prefixes;
#                                          see ENV PREFIXES below — every port must
#                                          implement them, none may ignore them
#   na.<plat> <id>   permanent|tooling <reason>
#   carveout.<plat> <id>  <test name>      by-name test exclusions, may be absent
#   setup     <plat> <description>         per-platform setup PHASE, not a step
#
# ENV PREFIXES ARE PART OF THE GRAMMAR, AND A PORT MUST IMPLEMENT THEM DELIBERATELY.
# A `cmd.<plat>` line may begin with one or more `NAME=VALUE ` pairs, applied to that
# command only. `scripts/pipeline.sh` gets this free from `eval "$cmd"`. NOTHING ELSE
# DOES: `packaging/windows/pipeline.ps1` dispatches through `cmd /c`, which has no such
# syntax and reads `G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals` as a program name — so the first line to
# use a prefix failed there with "'G_DEBUG' is not recognized", and the step whose whole
# purpose is to turn a Gtk-CRITICAL into a hard failure instead produced a hard failure
# no critical caused. A gate that cannot RUN is worse than one that is absent, because
# its red reads as a verdict.
#
# A port must strip the prefixes, apply them for the child, and RESTORE afterwards
# (including back to unset) so a step cannot leak an environment into later steps. Parse
# strictly — unquoted, space-free values, which is all this contract uses — and let
# anything else fall through and fail loudly rather than silently running a different
# command than the contract states.
#
# WHY THIS WENT UNCAUGHT, which is the more useful half: `--list-steps`/`-ListSteps`,
# `--self-test`/`-SelfTest` and the contract-mutation cases were ALL green, because not
# one of them EXECUTES a step. The self-tests validate the DERIVATION and never the
# EXECUTION, so both ports could be incapable of running a command and every check would
# still pass. Adding a grammar feature therefore obliges you to run it on every port,
# because nothing here will tell you it is unimplemented.
#
# SETUP IS A PHASE, NOT A STEP. Windows needs four environment variables before ANY step:
# PATH must carry the GTK bin for the runtime (a built binary will not start without it),
# PKG_CONFIG_PATH for the build probe, and LIB/INCLUDE for the MSVC link — the last two
# being conditional concatenations, because prepending onto an unset variable leaves a
# trailing `;` and an empty entry in those lists means "the current directory" to the
# toolchain. That produces environment, has no verdict, and cannot be skipped or
# reordered. Modelling it as "step 0" would force Linux and macOS to declare a
# non-applicable step 0 — noise that dilutes the declarations carrying real information.
# So it is declared here and MUST NOT appear in `--list-steps`.
#
# CARVE-OUTS ARE CONTRACT DATA, AND THE MECHANISM MUST SURVIVE BEING EMPTY. There are
# none today. That is exactly why the mechanism has to work while the list is empty —
# that is what lets a step print "no carve-outs" and have it MEAN something rather than
# be silence. Express them here rather than letting each runner invent its own.
#
# A carve-out is APPLIED, not merely announced. Each one appends `--skip <name>` to that
# platform's command, so the run does what the announcement says. The first version of
# this printed the list and ran the command unmodified — invisible while the list is
# empty, and the day someone added one the run would have printed `skipped: <test>` for
# a test it actually ran. An announcement that contradicts the run is worse than the
# silence the mechanism replaced.
# Because applying a carve-out means appending libtest arguments, a step that carries one
# must be a `cargo test` invocation, and the runners VALIDATE that rather than assuming
# it — an assumption that holds only until someone adds a carve-out to a step where it
# cannot mean anything.
#
# RUNNING THIS CONTRACT FROM POWERSHELL: do NOT use `Invoke-Expression` as the analogue
# of a POSIX `eval`. It throws on `cmd.windows diagram` — the outer shell expands the
# `$d` inside that double-quoted argument before the inner interpreter ever sees it
# ("The variable '$d' cannot be retrieved because it has not been set"). Dispatch via
# `cmd /c`, which does not treat `$` as special, and confirm the step still
# DISCRIMINATES (a malformed `<svg><g></svg>` must still fail it) rather than merely
# running. Measured on GTK 4.22.4 / Win10 by the Windows seat.
#
# CLASSES. `required` fails the run. `informational` never fails it. `review` is a human
# obligation with no command — announced in the run output so it cannot be silently
# forgotten, but not something a script can judge. Step 7 is the only `review` step and
# it is honest about that: pretending a script can decide whether a behaviour change
# carries its MANUAL-TEST.md check would be a gate that always passes. `packaging` is
# `required` that is also OPT-IN — it fails the run when asked for and does not run
# otherwise; see the paragraph on it below for why that is its own class rather than a
# flag on `required`.
#
# PROVENANCE TRAVELS WITH THE ARTEFACT. A packaging step is allowed to run in a run where
# a required step was skipped by operator override — `--skip-integration` plus `--package`
# is legitimate on a box where the GTK suite cannot run, and refusing the combination
# would be paternalistic about two flags the operator passed deliberately. But the
# packaging step MUST then name what did not run:
#
#     10. package — WARNING: built without step 5 (integration), skipped by --skip-integration
#
# so that whoever ends up holding the installer can tell what gated it. Silence here is
# the same failure as omitting a non-applicable step: the run looks identical to one where
# everything passed.
# Only OPERATOR OVERRIDES are reported, not contract-declared non-applicable steps. A step
# that is permanently non-applicable on a platform is always absent there, so naming it on
# every package is noise that trains the reader to skim past the line — and the line's
# whole value is that it appears only when something unusual happened.
#
# `packaging` is OPT-IN: skipped unless the runner is asked for it (`--package` /
# `-Package`), and required when it runs. Building installers on every change would add
# minutes to a gate meant to run after every edit, and the Windows pipeline already made
# this call before the contract existed — its staging and installer steps sit behind a
# `-Package` switch. The contract follows the existing behaviour rather than overriding
# it. A skipped packaging step is still ANNOUNCED, for the same reason a non-applicable
# one is: an omission the reader cannot see is how a step quietly stops existing.

platform linux    Linux
platform macos    macOS
platform windows  Windows

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 1 — formatting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   1  fmt
intent fmt  Every source file matches rustfmt's canonical formatting
verdict fmt  exit
class  fmt  required
cmd.linux   fmt  cargo fmt --check
cmd.macos   fmt  cargo fmt --check
cmd.windows fmt  cargo fmt --check

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 2 — lint
#
# The feature flag is not optional. Without it the gtk-integration-tests modules are not
# compiled at all, so neither clippy nor cargo test sees them and they rot unnoticed —
# they reached the point of NOT COMPILING while every gate stayed green, because a suite
# outside the gate protects nothing (ScrAP-124).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   2  clippy
intent clippy  The tree lints clean with zero warnings, with the GTK integration suite compiled in
verdict clippy  exit
class  clippy  required
cmd.linux   clippy  cargo clippy --all-targets --features gtk-integration-tests -- -D warnings
cmd.macos   clippy  cargo clippy --all-targets --features gtk-integration-tests -- -D warnings
cmd.windows clippy  cargo clippy --all-targets --features gtk-integration-tests -- -D warnings

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 3 — release build
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   3  build
intent build  The release binary compiles cleanly; release is the reference for behaviour and footprint
verdict build  exit
class  build  required
cmd.linux   build  cargo build --release
cmd.macos   build  cargo build --release
cmd.windows build  cargo build --release

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 4 — default test suite
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   4  test
intent test  The display-free test suite passes
verdict test  exit
class  test  required
cmd.linux   test  cargo test
cmd.macos   test  cargo test
cmd.windows test  cargo test

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 4b — skip report
#
# INFORMATIONAL BY DESIGN, and the reason is the whole point of the step. libtest
# CAPTURES a passing test's output, so a body that announces "I verified nothing" is
# silenced exactly when it passes. This step re-runs with output shown and greps for the
# marker, so a runtime skip is visible rather than indistinguishable from a pass.
# A skip nobody sees is a pass.
#
# The marker's label is the RUBRIC, not the test name, so renaming a test does not break
# the grep. Never `#[cfg(platform)]` a test to achieve a skip — a cfg'd-out test is not
# skipped, it is deleted: not compiled, not reported, not counted, and no harness
# distinguishes "never built" from "passed" (ScrAP-212).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   4b  skipreport
intent skipreport  Tests that skipped themselves at runtime are surfaced with their rubric and reason
verdict skipreport  marker:SKIPPED [
class  skipreport  informational
cmd.linux   skipreport  cargo test -- --nocapture
cmd.macos   skipreport  cargo test -- --nocapture
cmd.windows skipreport  cargo test -- --nocapture

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 5 — GTK integration tests
#
# The per-platform difference here is genuine and the contract expresses it rather than
# papering over it. Linux has no display in a headless run and supplies one with xvfb-run.
# macOS cannot use the `--lib` target at all: its dual-harness bodies abort the process
# off the main thread (ScrAP-171; measured 2026-07-30, GTK 4.22.4/Quartz — SIGABRT after
# ~95 non-GTK tests), so it runs the main-thread suite plus the standalone targets.
# Windows runs against the real windowing system.
#
# Run it through Cargo so .cargo/config.toml's [env] applies — these tests close real
# windows, which runs the production session-save path, and that path resolves
# XDG_STATE_HOME from the environment. Without the override the suite rewrites the
# tester's own session.toml.
#
# WINDOWS CARRIES `--test-threads=1` AND THE OTHER TWO DO NOT. This is a DIAGNOSABILITY
# measure, not a correctness one — `#[gtktest::test]` already serialises GTK bodies, so
# no platform needs it to pass. It is here because libtest buffers output per thread, so
# a parallel run of a suite that WEDGES prints no test names at all, and "zero test
# names" then reads as evidence the hang happened at or before the first test. That
# misreading cost a well-argued but wrong startup-stall hypothesis once already
# (ScrAP-166), on Windows, which is where a wedging GTK suite is most likely. It is
# scoped to the platform whose failure mode it serves rather than slowing the other two.
#
# LINUX AND WINDOWS CARRY `G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals`; macOS CANNOT. POLICY § Logging has
# always prescribed this flag for the test binary, so that a `Gtk-CRITICAL` becomes a hard
# failure instead of a silent log line — and until 2026-08-21 NOTHING IN THE TOOLCHAIN SET
# IT. A prescribed gate that is never armed cannot fail, and this one had not: the suite was
# carrying three latent criticals while reporting `ok` (a window built before
# `GApplication::startup`; a `compute_bounds` on a parentless popover; and a
# `change_action_state` on a STATELESS action, which was silently no-opping a guard's setup
# so it walked a smaller tree than it claimed to — ScrAP-209's own species, inside the test
# that cites it). All three are fixed and both Linux harnesses are clean under the flag.
# Windows confirmed its own suite clean under the flag and MUTATION-TESTED the gate rather
# than merely observing quiet: reverting one of the three fixes above makes the suite pass
# with the flag off and die with it on. macOS CANNOT carry it — an upstream GDK assertion
# fires on every popover open, with no application-side fix because the freeze/thaw calls
# are private and unbound, and masking that domain would also silence a latent defect in the
# same file at the version macOS ships (ScrAP-318).
#
# THE DEATH CODE DIFFERS BY PLATFORM AND A FAILURE IS EASY TO MISREAD. A promoted critical
# kills the process via `G_BREAKPOINT()`, which is `SIGTRAP` (exit 133) on Linux and
# `0xC0000409 STATUS_STACK_BUFFER_OVERRUN` — Windows' fast-fail — under MSVC, where the
# harness reports only "test exited abnormally". Anyone matching on `SIGTRAP` will misread a
# Windows failure as something else entirely (ScrAP-268 records the Linux half).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   5  integration
intent integration  The GTK integration suite passes against a real display, through Cargo so the [env] pins apply
verdict integration  exit
class  integration  required
cmd.linux   integration  G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals xvfb-run -a cargo test --features gtk-integration-tests
cmd.macos   integration  cargo test --features gtk-integration-tests --test gtk_suite --test icon_resolution --test macos_dark_mode --test popover_deferred_focus
cmd.windows integration  G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals cargo test --features gtk-integration-tests -- --test-threads=1
disarm.macos integration  permanent  G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals is absent from the macOS command above, and this line is why: GDK's Quartz backend freezes a surface once per CONSTRUCTION and thaws once per MAP, and gdk_macos_surface_hide never freezes, so the second map thaws at zero and the suite emits gdk_surface_thaw_updates assertions on every popover open or dismiss. Present 4.22.4 through main; the freeze/thaw pair is private and absent from the gdk4 bindings, so no application-side fix exists. Do NOT arm it by masking Gdk-CRITICAL: that would also silence ScrAP-318, a leaked update-freeze that stops a surface repainting, which is the only signal anybody would get if it happened. See also ScrAP-268 before reaching for a g_log_set_writer_func allowlist.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 6 — coverage ratchet
#
# Linux-canonical BY DESIGN. See the header's "record the semantic reason" note: the
# declarations below cite the semantics, never the missing tool.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   6  coverage
intent coverage  Scoped line coverage has not regressed below the ratchet floor
verdict coverage  exit
class  coverage  required
cmd.linux   coverage  scripts/coverage.sh
na.macos    coverage  permanent  src/platform/mac/*.rs compiles only here and is mostly GTK/CoreFoundation glue with no headless test, so it drags the scoped total below a Linux-calibrated floor. The floor's job is to gate Linux, where these files do not exist at all.
na.windows  coverage  permanent  Some unix-only code and tests (parts of atomic_io.rs, gated #[cfg(unix)] within a file that IS otherwise compiled here) are not built here, shifting both numerator and denominator, so a Windows figure can legitimately fall below the Linux floor with no regression. Never lower the Linux floor to make a Windows run pass.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 7 — UI-behaviour coverage alignment
#
# A `review` step: a human obligation, announced so it cannot be silently forgotten. No
# script can decide whether a change altered user-visible behaviour, and a gate that
# always passes is worse than an honest announcement.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   7  uibehaviour
intent uibehaviour  A change to user-visible behaviour carries its tests/MANUAL-TEST.md check, tracing to the sdd/TDD.md rubric it verifies
verdict uibehaviour  review
class  uibehaviour  review

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 8 — architecture diagram
#
# The Windows command is the worked example for intent-not-command. It uses a .NET
# built-in with no new dependency, and it DISCRIMINATES — fed `<svg><g></svg>` it rejects
# with a parse error, which was mutation-tested rather than assumed, because an XML check
# that accepts anything is worse than none.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   8  diagram
intent diagram  The architecture SVG parses as well-formed XML, so librsvg does not reject what a browser recovers from
verdict diagram  exit
class  diagram  required
cmd.linux   diagram  xmllint --noout sdd/system-overview.svg
cmd.macos   diagram  xmllint --noout sdd/system-overview.svg
cmd.windows diagram  powershell -NoProfile -Command "$d = New-Object System.Xml.XmlDocument; $d.Load('sdd/system-overview.svg')"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 9 — cross-reference gate
#
# A gate is its pattern AND the set of files it runs over, and parity is required in
# both. The two ports share one pattern, one self-test corpus, and one file enumeration
# (scripts/lint-references.scan) which both READ rather than restate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   9  references
intent references  Every cross-reference in the tree resolves, and the citation-form rules hold
verdict references  exit
class  references  required
cmd.linux   references  scripts/lint-references.sh
cmd.macos   references  scripts/lint-references.sh
cmd.windows references  powershell -NoProfile -File scripts/lint-references.ps1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 10 — the installer artefact
#
# ONE step, not a bundle/installer pair. Windows and macOS do stage-then-wrap in two
# phases; Linux's dpkg-deb and rpmbuild each stage and package in one call. Splitting
# the step to match two platforms would force the third to declare a non-applicable
# half, which is noise standing in for a distinction that is really about HOW each
# platform packages — exactly what `intent`-not-command exists to absorb.
#
# The intent is the property every platform must satisfy, and it is the one the plan
# names: an artefact a NON-DEVELOPER can install with NO toolchain. `install.sh` does
# not satisfy it and is not this step — it builds from source into ~/.local and needs
# cargo plus the -dev libraries, which makes it a developer convenience.
#
# VERSION COMES FROM Cargo.toml ON ALL THREE. A hand-maintained version string in a
# second place is the same defect class as everything else this contract prevents. Each
# platform derives it ONCE: Linux parses the [package] table, Windows reads it for the
# Inno Setup define, macOS reads it back out of the built .app's
# CFBundleShortVersionString rather than re-parsing Cargo.toml a second time.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
step   10  package
intent package  A transferable installer artefact a non-developer can install with no toolchain, versioned from Cargo.toml
verdict package  exit
class  package  packaging
cmd.linux   package  packaging/linux/build-deb.sh && packaging/linux/build-rpm.sh
cmd.macos   package  packaging/macos/dmg.sh
cmd.windows package  powershell -NoProfile -File packaging/windows/package.ps1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-platform setup phase (NOT steps — must not appear in --list-steps)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
setup linux    none
setup macos    none
setup windows  PATH (GTK bin, for the runtime), PKG_CONFIG_PATH (build probe), LIB and INCLUDE (MSVC link) — the last two conditionally concatenated, since prepending onto an unset variable leaves a trailing ';' and an empty entry means "the current directory" to the toolchain

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test carve-outs
#
# NONE TODAY. The mechanism must work while this list is empty — that is what lets step 5
# print "no carve-outs" and have it mean something rather than be silence. Add lines as
# `carveout.<platform>  integration  <exact test name>`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
